Estonian law says nothing about hitchhiking (''hääletamine''), so on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://www.riigiteataja.ee/akt/13335732?leiaKehtiv= Section 66 of the ''Liiklusseadus''] sets out what a ''kiirtee'' forbids, and the first item is pedestrians: no walking, no animals, no bicycles or mopeds, and no vehicle whose design speed is under 40 km/h. The second item forbids stopping or parking outside a designated area, and the rest rule out U-turns and reversing, so nobody can lawfully pull over for you or come back. That leaves the signposted rest areas and their petrol stations as the one place on a kiirtee where a lift can begin. Estonia has very little ''kiirtee'' and a great deal of ordinary trunk road, and on those hitchhiking is unregulated — stand on the verge where a driver can see you and stop safely.
